skill: "vertical-video-psychology"
sections[11]:
  - "\n"
  - "# Vertical Video Psychology & Editing Standards\n\nThis skill codifies 116 empirical research principles across cognitive psychology, perception, multimedia learning, and audience telemetry into actionable instructions for editing vertical video (YouTube Shorts, Instagram Reels, TikTok, and extracted clips).\n\n---\n\n"
  - "## When to Activate This Skill\n\nActivate this skill when:\n- Scripting, storyboarding, or assembling vertical short-form video (9:16 format).\n- Writing opening hooks, framing text overlays, or configuring on-screen safe zones.\n- Synchronizing speech, audio beds, sound effects, and multi-language captions.\n- Editing cuts, pacing event boundaries, or structuring technical tutorials.\n- Reviewing video drafts against cognitive load, retention curves, and empirical metrics.\n\n---\n\n"
  - "## The 10 Core Domains & Principle Index\n\nAll 116 empirical principles are documented in depth with direct study citations, edit directives, and experimental data across three reference volumes:\n\n1. **The Opening & Decision to Stay (Principles 1–11)**:\n   - Covers viewing motives, incidental discovery, goal-directed attention, onset capture, habituation control, information-gap curiosity, calibrated tease specificity, manageable novelty, expectation violation, and self-reference.\n   - *Reference*: [references/principles-01-to-34.md](references/principles-01-to-34.md)\n2. **Composition Inside a Vertical Frame (Principles 12–23)**:\n   - Covers portrait viewing effort, interface safe-zone clearance, figure-ground contrast, visual crowding mitigation, Gestalt proximity, similarity, common fate tracking, gaze cueing, inattentional blindness prevention, visual signaling, and WCAG redundant contrast coding.\n   - *Reference*: [references/principles-01-to-34.md](references/principles-01-to-34.md)\n3. **Voice, Captions, Music, and Timing (Principles 24–34)**:\n   - Covers synchronized verbatim captions, language matching, visible speech kinematics in noisy playback, manual gesture framing, prosodic grouping, lyric interference avoidance, temporal expectancy, acoustic credibility, playback speed bounds, and non-visual access.\n   - *Reference*: [references/principles-01-to-34.md](references/principles-01-to-34.md)\n4. **Cuts, Excerpts, and Story Structure (Principles 35–45)**:\n   - Covers action vector continuity across cuts, event boundary segmentation, situation models in interview excerpts, causal chain coherence, suspense design, retrospective curiosity, contextual juxtaposition (Kuleshov effect), character identification under constraints, and peak-end resolution.\n   - *Reference*: [references/principles-35-to-74.md](references/principles-35-to-74.md)\n5. **Comprehension, Learning, and Memory (Principles 46–64)**:\n   - Covers working-memory limits, spatial/temporal contiguity, stripping seductive details (e.g. background gameplay), dual coding, pictorial superiority, eliminating redundant verbal walls, pretraining, worked examples, prediction prompts, retrieval practice, and interleaving.\n   - *Reference*: [references/principles-35-to-74.md](references/principles-35-to-74.md)\n6. **Emotion, Humor, and Memory Priorities (Principles 65–74)**:\n   - Covers physiological arousal in transmission, arousal-biased memory filtering, benign violations in comedy, message-integrated humor, threat-and-efficacy pairing, precise problem framing, and processing fluency without superficiality.\n   - *Reference*: [references/principles-35-to-74.md](references/principles-35-to-74.md)\n7. **Trust, Persuasion, and Accurate Interpretation (Principles 75–87)**:\n   - Covers method disclosure over credentials, argument scrutiny on high-involvement issues, two-sided refutation, standardized denominator reporting, baseline anchoring, debunking via truth-repetition, causal replacements for retracted claims, and commercial sponsorship disclosure.\n   - *Reference*: [references/principles-75-to-116.md](references/principles-75-to-116.md)\n8. **Sharing, Communities, and Creator Relationships (Principles 88–99)**:\n   - Covers functional utility, recipient relevance, environmental memory triggers, blinded social proof, injunctive engineering norms, comment curation, parasocial continuity, remixable patterns, in-group jargon translation, and upfront reciprocity.\n   - *Reference*: [references/principles-75-to-116.md](references/principles-75-to-116.md)\n9. **Motivation, Choices, and Calls to Action (Principles 100–110)**:\n   - Covers autonomy support, progressive self-efficacy, implementation intentions (\"When X occurs, do Y\"), non-coercive language, choice scaffolding, goal proximity indicators, verified scarcity constraints, and loss-framed reference defaults.\n   - *Reference*: [references/principles-75-to-116.md](references/principles-75-to-116.md)\n10. **Feed Behavior, Switching, and Viewer Agency (Principles 111–116)**:\n    - Covers gaze patterns across split-screens, non-linear cut velocity calibration, eliminating cliffhanger fatigue, prospective memory cards, separating watch-time from retention, and relying on objective telemetry over self-report.\n    - *Reference*: [references/principles-75-to-116.md](references/principles-75-to-116.md)\n\n---\n\n"
  - "## Operational Editing Pipeline\n\nFollow this 5-stage pipeline when producing or editing vertical video:\n\n"
  - "### Stage 1: The Opening Architecture (First 3 Seconds)\n- Display the concrete noun and visible failure symptom in frame 1 [P02, P03].\n- Establish the baseline, then introduce the single anomaly [P10].\n- Eliminate generic throat-clearing, greetings, and ungrounded hyperbole [P01, P06].\n\n"
  - "### Stage 2: Dual-Track Safe-Zone Framing\n- Confine critical UI, demonstration assets, and captions to the central 1080x1400 safe area, keeping 200px top and 320px bottom margins clear of overlays [P13].\n- Write and verify the silent visual track first; ensure the full problem and solution are legible with volume muted [P24, P34].\n- Crop dense desktop interfaces down to the active 4–6 lines; eliminate visual crowding [P12, P15].\n\n"
  - "### Stage 3: Audio & Caption Engineering\n- Dialogue target: -14 LUFS integrated (-1.0 dB true peak) with zero room echo [P32].\n- Background music: strictly instrumental during instructional sequences; no conflicting vocal tracks [P29].\n- Captions: grouped into natural 2–4 word syntactic phrases with minimum 4.5:1 contrast backing [P22, P24]. Avoid single-word strobe tickers.\n\n"
  - "### Stage 4: Cognitive Pacing & Instruction\n- Remove seductive details: strip unrelated bottom-screen gameplay or decorative b-roll [P49].\n- Place labels directly adjacent to visual referents (spatial contiguity) [P47].\n- Synchronize spoken names with the exact frame of physical action (temporal contiguity) [P48].\n- Incorporate a 1-second pretesting pause before revealing the result [P57].\n\n"
  - "### Stage 5: Terminal Payoff & Call to Action\n- Conclude on the working, verified state [P44].\n- Frame the call to action as a conditional implementation intention: *\"Next time your build fails on cache, check this environment variable\"* [P103].\n- Hold a clean 2-second reference card displaying the exact command or syntax for prospective memory retention [P114].\n\n---\n\n"
  - "## Directives, Prompts & Worked Blueprints\n\n- **Production Prompts**: See [references/editing-prompts.md](references/editing-prompts.md) for ready-to-use system prompts for scriptwriters, timeline editors, caption mixers, and adversarial reviewers.\n- **Worked Blueprints**: See [references/worked-edits-and-measurement.md](references/worked-edits-and-measurement.md) for complete shot-by-shot blueprints across 4 archetypes (Tutorial, Podcast Clip, Visual Comedy, Product Review).\n- **Debunked Myths & A/B Testing**: See [references/worked-edits-and-measurement.md](references/worked-edits-and-measurement.md) for empirical analysis of creator myths and the 5-step scientific video testing protocol.\n"
